</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Large language models (LLMs) raise concerns about content authenticity and integrity because they can generate human-like text at scale. Text watermarks, which embed detectable statistical signals into generated text, offer a provable way to verify content origin. Many detection methods rely on pivotal statistics that are i.i.d. under human-written text, making goodness-of-fit (GoF) tests a natural tool for watermark detection. However, GoF tests remain largely underexplored in this setting.
In this paper, we systematically evaluate eight GoF tests across three popular watermarking schemes, using three open-source LLMs, two datasets, various generation temperatures, and multiple post-editing methods.
We find that general GoF tests can improve both the detection power and robustness of watermark detectors. Notably, we observe that text repetition, common in low-temperature settings, gives GoF tests a unique advantage not exploited by existing methods.
Our results highlight that classic GoF tests are a simple yet powerful and underused tool for watermark detection in LLMs.<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
          <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
          
          
          
          
          
          
          
        Code is available at <a href="https://github.com/hwq0726/GoF-for-Watermark-Detection" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/hwq0726/GoF-for-Watermark-Detection</a>.</span></span></span></p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h3>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The rapid advancement of large language models (LLMs) has enabled machines to generate fluent and coherent text that closely resembles human writing. While this progress has unlocked many applications, it has also raised significant concerns about the authenticity, ownership, and integrity of generated content. For example, LLMs can be misused to spread misinformation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>, <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>, <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite>, fabricate academic work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>, or manipulate digital communication at scale <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>. These risks have prompted growing interest in developing reliable tools to trace and verify machine-generated text.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A promising solution is text watermarking, which embeds hidden statistical signals into generated text. Specically, it introduces dependencies between tokens <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{w}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{w}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and secret pseudorandom numbers <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. In watermarked generation, each token <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{w}_{t}\sim\bm{P}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>𝑷</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{w}_{t}\sim\bm{P}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is produced by a decoding function <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{w}_{t}={\mathcal{S}}(\bm{P}_{t},\zeta_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝑷</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{w}_{t}={\mathcal{S}}(\bm{P}_{t},\zeta_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{P}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑷</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{P}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is the next-token predictive (NTP) distribution that <math id="S1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{w}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{w}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> follows. This induces a watermark signal—statistical dependence between <math id="S1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{w}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{w}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. In contrast, human-written text lacks this dependence, as humans do not have access to <math id="S1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. Detection methods leverage this difference using scalar pivotal statistics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>, defined as <math id="S1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{t}=Y({w}_{t},\zeta_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{t}=Y({w}_{t},\zeta_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, within a hypothesis testing framework:</p>
<table id="S1.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H_{0}:{w}_{1:n}~\text{is human-written}\quad\text{vs.}\quad H_{1}:{w}_{1:n}~\text{is LLM-generated}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>w</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mtext>is human-written</mtext><mtext>vs.</mtext><mi>H</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>w</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mtext>is LLM-generated</mtext><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{0}:{w}_{1:n}~\text{is human-written}\quad\text{vs.}\quad H_{1}:{w}_{1:n}~\text{is LLM-generated}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">A key property of the pivotal function <math id="S1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is that <math id="S1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y({w},\zeta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y({w},\zeta)</annotation></semantics></math> follows a known distribution <math id="S1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> whenever <math id="S1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> is independent of <math id="S1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ζ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta</annotation></semantics></math>, which exactly holds for human-written <math id="S1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{w}</annotation></semantics></math> since human users do not know the secret <math id="S1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ζ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, problem (<a href="#S1.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On the Empirical Power of Goodness-of-Fit Tests in Watermark Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) can be reformulated in terms of <math id="S1.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S1.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{0}:Y_{t}\sim\mu_{0}\text{ i.i.d.}\quad\text{vs.}\quad H_{1}:Y_{t}\sim\text{other distribution that depends on}~\bm{P}_{t}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> i.i.d.</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>vs.</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mtext>other distribution that depends on</mtext><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑷</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{0}:Y_{t}\sim\mu_{0}\text{ i.i.d.}\quad\text{vs.}\quad H_{1}:Y_{t}\sim\text{other distribution that depends on}~\bm{P}_{t}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The formulation in (<a href="#S1.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On the Empirical Power of Goodness-of-Fit Tests in Watermark Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) is standard and popular in the watermarking literature <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>, <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>]</cite>. Many detection rules, though somewhat ad hoc, are designed to address this problem. In the idealized setting where all NTP distributions <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\bm{P}_{t}\}_{t=1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝑷</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\bm{P}_{t}\}_{t=1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> are known, the log-likelihood ratio test based on the pivotal statistics <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{Y_{t}\}_{t=1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{Y_{t}\}_{t=1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is provably optimal <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>. In practice, however, these distributions are often unavailable due to limited access to commercial LLMs and unknown prompts. To overcome this, recent works have developed NTP-agnostic approaches. For example, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. [30]</a></cite> assumes all <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{P}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑷</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{P}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> lie within a known class and derives the least favorable detection rule using a minimax formulation. This framework is extended by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. [31]</a></cite> to handle human editing, where the observed text may contain a mix of human-written and LLM-generated content. In this case, the alternative hypothesis in (<a href="#S1.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On the Empirical Power of Goodness-of-Fit Tests in Watermark Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) is modified to reflect the fraction of human-edited tokens. Nonetheless, both works share the same null hypothesis <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{Y_{t}\}_{t=1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{Y_{t}\}_{t=1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> are i.i.d. samples from the known <math id="S1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This structure reveals a close connection between watermark detection and the classical statistical problem of goodness-of-fit (GoF) testing. GoF tests aim to determine whether i.i.d. samples follow a given distribution. While watermark detection involves non-i.i.d. data under <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>—due to the autoregressive nature of LLMs, where each <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{P}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑷</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{P}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> depends on the history <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{w}_{1:(t-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{w}_{1:(t-1)}</annotation></semantics></math>—GoF tests remain applicable because <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is the same: <math id="S1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{Y_{t}\}_{t=1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{Y_{t}\}_{t=1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> are i.i.d. from a known <math id="S1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Despite their long-standing role in statistics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">GoF methods have received limited attention in the watermarking literature</span>.
Prior work has largely focused on designing new watermarking schemes, with less emphasis on improving detection power (see Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Related Work ‣ Appendices ‣ On the Empirical Power of Goodness-of-Fit Tests in Watermark Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> for a detailed discussion).
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. [31]</a></cite> proposed a truncated divergence-based GoF test and analyzed its theoretical performance on the Gumbel-max watermark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, but its effectiveness on other watermarking schemes remains unclear, as does the performance of other classical GoF tests.
This raises a natural question: <span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">how well do classical GoF tests work for modern watermark detection?</span>
A systematic study of classical GoF tests could provide valuable insights for developing robust and effective watermark detection methods.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Our contribution.</h5>

<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we study this connection empirically, and our contributions are:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Systematic empirical evaluation.</span>
We introduce general GoF tests to watermark detection and systematically evaluate eight GoF detection rules across three popular watermarking schemes. Our experiments span diverse configurations, including three open-source language models, two datasets, four temperature settings, and three types of edits.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Understanding GoF advantages.</span>
GoF tests consistently outperform baseline methods across varying temperatures and text lengths, and this robustness stems from their ability to capture distribution-level deviations in the pivotal statistics.
At high temperatures, watermark signals are stronger due to increased entropy in the next-token distributions. This leads to more noticeable shifts between the empirical CDF and the null CDF, which GoF tests are particularly effective at detecting—even with moderate text lengths.
At low temperatures, watermark signals tend to weaken, making detection more challenging. However, lower temperatures also induce repetition in the generated text, introducing structured patterns that GoF tests can exploit. This repetition results in deviations from the expected null CDF, enabling GoF tests to maintain strong detection power even in low-entropy settings.
These complementary advantages explain why GoF tests are effective across a wide range of generation scenarios, including those that are traditionally hard for watermark detection.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Robustness to modification.</span>
We evaluate GoF tests under both common text edits (deletion and substitution) and information-rich edits. In all cases, GoF-based methods maintain high detection power, demonstrating strong robustness to modifications.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">In short, our results show that GoF tests are a valuable complement to existing watermark detection methods, improving both detection power and robustness across a wide range of conditions.
Due to space constraints, we defer discussion of related work to Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Related Work ‣ Appendices ‣ On the Empirical Power of Goodness-of-Fit Tests in Watermark Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Preliminaries</h3>

<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Watermark embedding.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">LLMs like the GPT series <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> generate text autoregressively, producing one token at a time based on previous tokens. Each token is drawn from a fixed vocabulary <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathcal{W}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathcal{W}}}</annotation></semantics></math> using a multinomial distribution <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{P}_{t}=(P_{t,w})_{w\in{{\mathcal{W}}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑷</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{P}_{t}=(P_{t,w})_{w\in{{\mathcal{W}}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, called the next-token prediction (NTP) distribution. This distribution depends on the previous tokens, user prompts, and system-level prompts, summarized as <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bm{P}_{t}=\mathcal{M}({w}_{1:(t-1)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑷</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{P}_{t}=\mathcal{M}({w}_{1:(t-1)})</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>, <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>.
To embed a watermark, the model uses a pseudorandom variable <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, computed by a deterministic hash function:
<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\zeta_{t}={\mathcal{A}}({w}_{(t-m):(t-1)},{\mathtt{Key}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝙺𝚎𝚢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta_{t}={\mathcal{A}}({w}_{(t-m):(t-1)},{\mathtt{Key}})</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a hash function, <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> is a context window size, and <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathtt{Key}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝙺𝚎𝚢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathtt{Key}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a secret key. Once <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is computed, the next token is generated as <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{w}_{t}={\mathcal{S}}(\bm{P}_{t},\zeta_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝑷</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{w}_{t}={\mathcal{S}}(\bm{P}_{t},\zeta_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a (possibly stochastic) decoder.
The decoder <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math> is said to be unbiased if for any <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑷</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{P}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{w}</annotation></semantics></math>, it satisfies <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}_{\zeta\sim\pi}({\mathcal{S}}(\bm{P},\zeta)={w})=P_{{w}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑷</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}_{\zeta\sim\pi}({\mathcal{S}}(\bm{P},\zeta)={w})=P_{{w}}</annotation></semantics></math>. This means that when <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ζ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta</annotation></semantics></math> is truly random, <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}(\bm{P},\zeta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑷</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}(\bm{P},\zeta)</annotation></semantics></math> still follows the original NTP distribution <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑷</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{P}</annotation></semantics></math>. In other words, an unbiased decoder samples from the intended NTP.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Watermark detection.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As introduced in Section <a href="#S1" title="1 Introduction ‣ On the Empirical Power of Goodness-of-Fit Tests in Watermark Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, watermark detection aims to identify statistical dependence between each token <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{w}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{w}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and the pseudorandom number <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. This is done using pivotal statistics, leading to the hypothesis testing problem in (<a href="#S1.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On the Empirical Power of Goodness-of-Fit Tests in Watermark Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>. Under <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, there is no watermark, and <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{w}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{w}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> are assumed to be independent. By design, the pivotal statistic <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{t}=Y({w}_{t},\zeta_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{t}=Y({w}_{t},\zeta_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> then follows a known distribution <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, regardless of how <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{w}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{w}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is distributed.
Under the alternative, the watermarking process introduces dependence through the decoder <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math>, causing <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> to deviate from <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{t}\mid\bm{P}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝑷</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{t}\mid\bm{P}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> follows a distribution <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{1,\bm{P}_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝑷</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{1,\bm{P}_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math> that depends only on <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{P}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑷</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{P}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. This leads to the following hypothesis testing problem:</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{0}:Y_{t}\sim\mu_{0}\text{ i.i.d.}\quad\text{vs.}\quad H_{1}:Y_{t}\mid\bm{P}_{t}\sim\mu_{1,\bm{P}_{t}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> i.i.d.</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>vs.</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑷</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝑷</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{0}:Y_{t}\sim\mu_{0}\text{ i.i.d.}\quad\text{vs.}\quad H_{1}:Y_{t}\mid\bm{P}_{t}\sim\mu_{1,\bm{P}_{t}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. [31]</a></cite> extend it to a more practical setting where the generated text may be partially edited. They model human edits as a mixture effect so that <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is from a mixture of <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{1,\bm{P}_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝑷</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{1,\bm{P}_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math> under <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Three considered unbiased watermarks.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider three unbiased watermarking schemes. For brevity, we describe only their null distributions <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, which suffice for understanding our methods and results. Full details are provided in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Introduction of Considered Watermarks ‣ Appendices ‣ On the Empirical Power of Goodness-of-Fit Tests in Watermark Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.
The first is the Gumbel-max watermark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, where <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is the uniform distribution <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{U}(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{U}(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>.
The second is the inverse transform watermark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>, whose null CDF satisfies <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}(Y\leq r)=r^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}(Y\leq r)=r^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.
The third is Google’s SynthID watermark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, where the pivotal statistic under <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> follows the distribution of <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{k}\mathrm{IrwinHall}(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>IrwinHall</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{k}\mathrm{IrwinHall}(k)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{IrwinHall}(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>IrwinHall</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{IrwinHall}(k)</annotation></semantics></math> is the Irwin–Hall distribution, i.e., the sum of <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> i.i.d. <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{U}(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{U}(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> variables.
A common feature of all three is that their null distributions <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> are known and have computable CDFs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">An exception.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The green-red list watermark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>]</cite> (see Appendix <a href="#A1.SS4" title="A.4 Green-red List Watermark ‣ Appendix A Introduction of Considered Watermarks ‣ Appendices ‣ On the Empirical Power of Goodness-of-Fit Tests in Watermark Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.4</span></a>) is a popular method. It randomly partitions the vocabulary into a green (favored) and red (disfavored) set, boosts the probabilities of green tokens, and samples from the perturbed NTP distribution. Detection is based on counting green tokens and rejecting <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> if their frequency exceeds a threshold. Variants <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> use more carefully designed partitions but follow the same overall pipeline.
We exclude these methods from our experiments because GoF tests reduce to the original detection rule in this setting. The pivotal statistic is binary (whether a token is green), and the order of them does not matter—only the total count of green tokens is informative. As a result, both methods are effectively equivalent, making additional experiments unnecessary.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>GoF Tests for Watermark Detection</h3>

<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Rationale for using GoF tests.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Goodness-of-fit (GoF) tests are classical tools in statistics for determining whether a sequence of i.i.d. samples comes from a specified distribution <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>. The standard formulation is:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E4X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle H_{0}:Y_{t}\sim\mu_{0}~~\text{i.i.d.}~~\forall t\quad\text{vs.}\quad H_{1}^{\mathrm{standard}}:Y_{t}\sim\mu_{1}~~\text{i.i.d.}~~\forall t," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>i.i.d.</mtext><mo lspace="0.827em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>vs.</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>standard</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>i.i.d.</mtext><mo lspace="0.827em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle H_{0}:Y_{t}\sim\mu_{0}~~\text{i.i.d.}~~\forall t\quad\text{vs.}\quad H_{1}^{\mathrm{standard}}:Y_{t}\sim\mu_{1}~~\text{i.i.d.}~~\forall t,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{1}\neq\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{1}\neq\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. GoF tests evaluate deviations from the null distribution <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and determine whether they are statistically significant.
However, this classical setup does not directly apply to watermark detection, where the alternative hypothesis <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S2.E3" title="Equation 3 ‣ Watermark detection. ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ On the Empirical Power of Goodness-of-Fit Tests in Watermark Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) involves structured dependence introduced by the watermarking process. Specifically, the pivotal statistics <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{Y_{t}\}_{t=1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{Y_{t}\}_{t=1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> are not i.i.d. under <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> due to the autoregressive nature of LLMs and the time-varying NTP distributions <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{P}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑷</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{P}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, the core principle of GoF tests—measuring deviation from the null distribution <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>—remains highly relevant. The most related work, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. [31]</a></cite>, proposed a divergence-based GoF test <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> for detecting Gumbel-max watermarks and demonstrated its asymptotic robustness. Yet, they did not establish its uniqueness or compare it against other GoF tests. Moreover, their analysis relies on asymptotic assumptions such as infinite text length, while in practice, the finite-sample performance of GoF tests can vary substantially—even among asymptotically optimal methods.
Our work complements this line of research by empirically evaluating classical GoF tests in the context of watermark detection. We also investigate which factors most affect their performance.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Introduction of GoF tests.</h5>

<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S3.T1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="S3.T1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Considered GoF tests.</span></figcaption>
<div id="S3.T1.5" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:53.8pt;vertical-align:-25.8pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-211.2pt,32.9pt) scale(0.449599096773893,0.449599096773893) ;">
<table id="S3.T1.5.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S3.T1.5.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt"><span id="S3.T1.5.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Tests name</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.5.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt"><span id="S3.T1.5.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Deviation measure</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.5.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt"><span id="S3.T1.5.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Tests name</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.5.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="S3.T1.5.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Deviation measure</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.5.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.5.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T1.5.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span> <span id="S3.T1.5.1.2.1.2" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S3.T1.5.1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span id="S3.T1.5.1.2.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.5.1.2.1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">Tr-GoF test,</span></span>
<span id="S3.T1.5.1.2.1.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.5.1.2.1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center"><span id="S3.T1.5.1.2.1.2.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Phi</span>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite></span></span>
</span></span><span id="S3.T1.5.1.2.1.3" class="ltx_text"></span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.5.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><math id="S3.T1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{n}^{+}(s)=\sup\limits_{r\in[{\textnormal{p}}^{+},1)}K_{s}^{+}(F_{n}(r),F_{0}(r))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mtext>p</mtext><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{n}^{+}(s)=\sup\limits_{r\in[{\textnormal{p}}^{+},1)}K_{s}^{+}(F_{n}(r),F_{0}(r))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T1.5.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T1.5.1.2.3.1" class="ltx_text"></span> <span id="S3.T1.5.1.2.3.2" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S3.T1.5.1.2.3.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span id="S3.T1.5.1.2.3.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.5.1.2.3.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">Kuiper’s test,</span></span>
<span id="S3.T1.5.1.2.3.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.5.1.2.3.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center"><span id="S3.T1.5.1.2.3.2.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Kui</span>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite></span></span>
</span></span><span id="S3.T1.5.1.2.3.3" class="ltx_text"></span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.5.1.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S3.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{n}=\max\limits_{1\leq i\leq n}\left(\frac{i}{n}-{\textnormal{p}}_{(i)}\right)+\max\limits_{1\leq i\leq n}\left({\textnormal{p}}_{(i)}-\frac{i-1}{n}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo>−</mo><msub><mtext>p</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>p</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{n}=\max\limits_{1\leq i\leq n}\left(\frac{i}{n}-{\textnormal{p}}_{(i)}\right)+\max\limits_{1\leq i\leq n}\left({\textnormal{p}}_{(i)}-\frac{i-1}{n}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.5.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.5.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T1.5.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span> <span id="S3.T1.5.1.3.1.2" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S3.T1.5.1.3.1.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span id="S3.T1.5.1.3.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.5.1.3.1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">Kolmogorov-Smirnov test</span></span>
<span id="S3.T1.5.1.3.1.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.5.1.3.1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center"><span id="S3.T1.5.1.3.1.2.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Kol</span>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite></span></span>
</span></span><span id="S3.T1.5.1.3.1.3" class="ltx_text"></span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.5.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><math id="S3.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{n}=\max\limits_{1\leq i\leq n}\left\{\max\left({\textnormal{p}}_{(i)}-\frac{i-1}{n},\frac{i}{n}-{\textnormal{p}}_{(i)}\right)\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>p</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo>−</mo><msub><mtext>p</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{n}=\max\limits_{1\leq i\leq n}\left\{\max\left({\textnormal{p}}_{(i)}-\frac{i-1}{n},\frac{i}{n}-{\textnormal{p}}_{(i)}\right)\right\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T1.5.1.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T1.5.1.3.3.1" class="ltx_text"></span> <span id="S3.T1.5.1.3.3.2" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S3.T1.5.1.3.3.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span id="S3.T1.5.1.3.3.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.5.1.3.3.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">Anderson-Darling test,</span></span>
<span id="S3.T1.5.1.3.3.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.5.1.3.3.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center"><span id="S3.T1.5.1.3.3.2.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">And</span>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite></span></span>
</span></span><span id="S3.T1.5.1.3.3.3" class="ltx_text"></span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.5.1.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T1.5.1.3.4.1" class="ltx_text"></span><span id="S3.T1.5.1.3.4.2" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S3.T1.5.1.3.4.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span id="S3.T1.5.1.3.4.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.5.1.3.4.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.T1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="A^{2}=-n-\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}(2i-1)\log\big[" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>log</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{2}=-n-\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}(2i-1)\log\big[</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>
<span id="S3.T1.5.1.3.4.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.5.1.3.4.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.T1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\qquad\qquad(1-{\textnormal{p}}_{(n+1-i)}){\textnormal{p}}_{(i)}\big]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mtext>p</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mtext>p</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\qquad\qquad(1-{\textnormal{p}}_{(n+1-i)}){\textnormal{p}}_{(i)}\big]</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>
</span></span><span id="S3.T1.5.1.3.4.3" class="ltx_text"></span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.5.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.5.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T1.5.1.4.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span> <span id="S3.T1.5.1.4.1.2" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S3.T1.5.1.4.1.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span id="S3.T1.5.1.4.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.5.1.4.1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">Cramér-von Mises test,</span></span>
<span id="S3.T1.5.1.4.1.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.5.1.4.1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center"><span id="S3.T1.5.1.4.1.2.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Cra</span>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite></span></span>
</span></span><span id="S3.T1.5.1.4.1.3" class="ltx_text"></span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.5.1.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><math id="S3.T1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{2}=\frac{1}{12n}+\sum_{i=1}^{n}\left({\textnormal{p}}_{(i)}-\frac{2i-1}{2n}\right)^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>12</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>p</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{2}=\frac{1}{12n}+\sum_{i=1}^{n}\left({\textnormal{p}}_{(i)}-\frac{2i-1}{2n}\right)^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T1.5.1.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T1.5.1.4.3.1" class="ltx_text"></span> <span id="S3.T1.5.1.4.3.2" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S3.T1.5.1.4.3.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span id="S3.T1.5.1.4.3.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.5.1.4.3.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">Watson’s test,</span></span>
<span id="S3.T1.5.1.4.3.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.5.1.4.3.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center"><span id="S3.T1.5.1.4.3.2.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Wat</span>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite></span></span>
</span></span><span id="S3.T1.5.1.4.3.3" class="ltx_text"></span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.5.1.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S3.T1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{2}=W^{2}-n\left(\bar{F}-\frac{1}{2}\right)^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{2}=W^{2}-n\left(\bar{F}-\frac{1}{2}\right)^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.5.1.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.5.1.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T1.5.1.5.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span> <span id="S3.T1.5.1.5.1.2" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S3.T1.5.1.5.1.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span id="S3.T1.5.1.5.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.5.1.5.1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">Neyman’s smooth test,</span></span>
<span id="S3.T1.5.1.5.1.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.5.1.5.1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center"><span id="S3.T1.5.1.5.1.2.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Ney</span>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite></span></span>
</span></span><span id="S3.T1.5.1.5.1.3" class="ltx_text"></span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.5.1.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><math id="S3.T1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{k}=n\sum_{j=1}^{k}a_{j}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{k}=n\sum_{j=1}^{k}a_{j}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T1.5.1.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T1.5.1.5.3.1" class="ltx_text"></span> <span id="S3.T1.5.1.5.3.2" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S3.T1.5.1.5.3.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span id="S3.T1.5.1.5.3.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.5.1.5.3.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">Chi-squared test,</span></span>
<span id="S3.T1.5.1.5.3.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.5.1.5.3.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center"><span id="S3.T1.5.1.5.3.2.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Chi</span>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite></span></span>
</span></span><span id="S3.T1.5.1.5.3.3" class="ltx_text"></span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.5.1.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><math id="S3.T1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}=\sum_{i=1}^{k}\frac{(O_{i}-E_{i})^{2}}{E_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}=\sum_{i=1}^{k}\frac{(O_{i}-E_{i})^{2}}{E_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All GoF tests evaluate deviations from the null distribution <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and reject <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> if the deviation exceeds a specified threshold. Thus, it suffices to define the measures of deviation used by each test.
Before doing so, we introduce some notation. Let <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{1},\ldots,Y_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{1},\ldots,Y_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the pivotal statistics, and let <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{(1)}\leq Y_{(2)}\leq\dots\leq Y_{(n)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{(1)}\leq Y_{(2)}\leq\dots\leq Y_{(n)}</annotation></semantics></math> be their order statistics. The empirical cumulative distribution function (CDF) is defined as <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{n}(r)=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{t=1}^{n}{\bf 1}_{Y_{t}\leq r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{n}(r)=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{t=1}^{n}{\bf 1}_{Y_{t}\leq r}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the theoretical CDF under the null is <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{0}(r)=\mu_{0}(Y\leq r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{0}(r)=\mu_{0}(Y\leq r)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Recall that the null distribution <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is known but varies by watermarking scheme (see Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Preliminaries ‣ On the Empirical Power of Goodness-of-Fit Tests in Watermark Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). Given this, we can also compute <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-values as <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\textnormal{p}}_{t}=1-F_{0}(Y_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>p</mtext><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\textnormal{p}}_{t}=1-F_{0}(Y_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Eight GoF tests are summarized in Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Introduction of GoF tests. ‣ 3 GoF Tests for Watermark Detection ‣ On the Empirical Power of Goodness-of-Fit Tests in Watermark Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, with each referred to by a three-letter abbreviation (e.g., the Kolmogorov-Smirnov test as <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Kol</span>). All GoF tests operate on pivotal statistics and are permutation-invariant. Therefore, the order of pivotal statistics does not affect their detection results. Below, we provide additional details omitted from the table for brevity:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Tr-GoF test <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite> uses a truncated <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>-divergence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>, which is the reason for its name <span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_typewriter">Phi</span>. The truncation is introduced via <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{n}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{n}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\textnormal{p}}^{+}=\sup\{{\textnormal{p}}_{(t)}:{\textnormal{p}}_{(t)}\leq c_{n}^{+}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>p</mtext><mo>+</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">sup</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mtext>p</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>p</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\textnormal{p}}^{+}=\sup\{{\textnormal{p}}_{(t)}:{\textnormal{p}}_{(t)}\leq c_{n}^{+}\}</annotation></semantics></math> to ensure numerical stability.
Here, <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="K_{s}^{+}(u,v)=K_{s}(u,v){\bf 1}\{0&lt;v&lt;u&lt;1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{s}^{+}(u,v)=K_{s}(u,v){\bf 1}\{0&lt;v&lt;u&lt;1\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{s}(u,v)=v\phi_{s}\left(\frac{u}{v}\right)+(1-v)\phi_{s}\left(\frac{1-u}{1-v}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{s}(u,v)=v\phi_{s}\left(\frac{u}{v}\right)+(1-v)\phi_{s}\left(\frac{1-u}{1-v}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> is the untruncated <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>-diveregence.
The function <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{s}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{s}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is convex and defined as:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{s}(x)=\begin{cases}x\log x-x+1,&amp;\text{if }s=1,\\
\frac{1-s+sx-x^{s}}{s(1-s)},&amp;\text{if }s\neq 0,1,\\
-\log x+x-1,&amp;\text{if }s=0.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>s</mi></msup></mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{s}(x)=\begin{cases}x\log x-x+1,&amp;\text{if }s=1,\\
\frac{1-s+sx-x^{s}}{s(1-s)},&amp;\text{if }s\neq 0,1,\\
-\log x+x-1,&amp;\text{if }s=0.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">In our experiment, we fix <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=2</annotation></semantics></math> for all considered watermarks due to its good performance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Kolmogorov-Smirnov test (<span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Kol</span>) measures the largest difference between the empirical and null CDFs. In fact, one can show that <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{n}=\sup_{x}|F_{n}(x)-F_{0}(x)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">sup</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{n}=\sup_{x}|F_{n}(x)-F_{0}(x)|</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Watson’s test adjusts the Cramér-von Mises statistic <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> to ensure invariance to location changes, so <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{2}=W^{2}-n\left(\bar{F}-\frac{1}{2}\right)^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{2}=W^{2}-n\left(\bar{F}-\frac{1}{2}\right)^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{F}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}F(Y_{(i)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{F}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}F(Y_{(i)})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Neyman’s smooth test (<span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Ney</span>) expands deviations from the null distribution using orthonormal polynomials. Specifically, it computes coefficients</p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{\lambda_{j}}}\sum_{i=1}^{n}h_{j}\left(Y_{i}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msqrt></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{\lambda_{j}}}\sum_{i=1}^{n}h_{j}\left(Y_{i}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.I1.i4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> are orthonormal Legendre polynomials for <math id="S3.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.I1.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> are normalizing constants. The test statistic then aggregates the first <math id="S3.I1.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> coefficients as <math id="S3.I1.i4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{k}=n\sum_{j=1}^{k}a_{j}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{k}=n\sum_{j=1}^{k}a_{j}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S3.I1.i4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=3</annotation></semantics></math> used in our experiments.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Chi-squared test (<span id="S3.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Chi</span>) divides the range of <math id="S3.I1.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S3.I1.i5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> equal-width bins, counts observed frequencies <math id="S3.I1.i5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and computes expected frequencies <math id="S3.I1.i5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{i}=n/k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{i}=n/k</annotation></semantics></math>. The test statistic is <math id="S3.I1.i5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}=\sum_{i=1}^{k}\frac{(O_{i}-E_{i})^{2}}{E_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}=\sum_{i=1}^{k}\frac{(O_{i}-E_{i})^{2}}{E_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> with critical values drawn from the chi-squared distribution with <math id="S3.I1.i5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k-1</annotation></semantics></math> degrees of freedom <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Importantly, for most GoF tests, the exact null distribution does not admit a closed-form expression; instead, only an asymptotic distribution emerges as the token length <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> grows. Nevertheless, our experiments show that relying on this large-sample approximation still yields reliable Type I error control in practice. We defer a detailed discussion of their practical considerations to Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Practical Considerations for GoF Tests ‣ Appendices ‣ On the Empirical Power of Goodness-of-Fit Tests in Watermark Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.
To conclude, we integrate the full detection procedure into Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ Introduction of GoF tests. ‣ 3 GoF Tests for Watermark Detection ‣ On the Empirical Power of Goodness-of-Fit Tests in Watermark Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, providing a streamlined view of how the Kolmogorov–Smirnov test (<span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Kol</span>) can be applied to the Gumbel-max watermark.
The same procedure can be straightforwardly applied to other GoF tests, which readers may extend as needed.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> GoF test for watermark detection (example: <span id="alg1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Kol</span> for the Gumbel–max watermark)</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.6" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
              
            Token sequence <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{w}_{1:n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{w}_{1:n}</annotation></semantics></math>; watermark decoder <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math>; significance level <math id="alg1.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>; CDF under the null <math id="alg1.l1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Compute pivotal statistics</span> <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{1},\ldots,Y_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{1},\ldots,Y_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> from the sequence <math id="alg1.l2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{w}_{1:n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{w}_{1:n}</annotation></semantics></math>. 
</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Compute <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-values:</span> <math id="alg1.l3.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\textnormal{p}}_{t}=1-F_{0}(Y_{t}),\;t=1,\ldots,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>p</mtext><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\textnormal{p}}_{t}=1-F_{0}(Y_{t}),\;t=1,\ldots,n</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sort</span> the <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-values in ascending order: <math id="alg1.l4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\textnormal{p}}_{(1)}\leq\cdots\leq{\textnormal{p}}_{(n)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>p</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><msub><mtext>p</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\textnormal{p}}_{(1)}\leq\cdots\leq{\textnormal{p}}_{(n)}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Compute the test statistic (<span id="alg1.l5.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Kol</span>)</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
              
              
               <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{n}\leftarrow\max_{1\leq i\leq n}\max\!\Big({\textnormal{p}}_{(i)}-\frac{i-1}{n},\,\frac{i}{n}-{\textnormal{p}}_{(i)}\Big)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.808em;" width="1.808em"><mi>max</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>p</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo>−</mo><msub><mtext>p</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{n}\leftarrow\max_{1\leq i\leq n}\max\!\Big({\textnormal{p}}_{(i)}-\frac{i-1}{n},\,\frac{i}{n}-{\textnormal{p}}_{(i)}\Big).</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Estimate critical value</span> <math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> based on the information of the watermarking scheme.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
              
              
